# Go patterns

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic design guidance to idiomatic
Go design and its common anti-patterns. Applies only to `*.go` files
(W1-AC8).

## Module and package layout

- `cmd/<binary>/` holds one `main` package per executable; `internal/`
  holds packages this module's own code may import but nothing outside
  the module can — put anything not meant as a public API surface there.
- A package should have a clear, single responsibility; a package that
  only holds shared types with no behavior ("models", "types", "common")
  invites import cycles as the project grows — prefer colocating a type
  with the package that owns its behavior.
- Avoid import cycles by depth, not by breaking them with an `internal`
  workaround package — if package A needs package B's type and B needs
  A's, one of the two owns too much; extract the shared piece into a
  third package both depend on.

## Idiomatic concurrency patterns

- Fan-out/fan-in: bound the number of concurrent workers (a worker pool or
  `golang.org/x/sync/errgroup`'s `SetLimit`), never spawn one goroutine per
  input item on an unbounded input source.
- Use `errgroup.Group` when a set of goroutines shares one cancellation
  and you need the first error; use a plain `sync.WaitGroup` when you only
  need to know they finished and errors are handled another way.
- Prefer channels for signaling and handoff, mutexes for protecting shared
  state — do not build a hand-rolled semaphore out of a channel when
  `sync.Mutex` (or `errgroup.SetLimit`) already says what you mean.
- Close a channel only from the sender, never the receiver, and only
  once; a `nil` channel blocks forever on send/receive, which is
  occasionally the correct way to disable a `select` case.

## Composition over inheritance

- Use struct embedding to reuse behavior only when the embedding type
  genuinely "is-a" superset of the embedded one's API; prefer an explicit
  field plus delegation when you would otherwise promote methods the
  caller should not see.
- Functional options (`func WithTimeout(d time.Duration) Option`) for a
  constructor with many optional parameters, instead of a boolean-heavy
  positional signature or a half-filled config struct.

## Anti-patterns to flag

- A large `interface{}`/`any`-typed function signature where a generic
  type parameter or a smaller concrete interface would give compile-time
  safety back.
- A "god package" (`utils`, `helpers`, `common`) that accumulates
  unrelated functions — split by what the code actually does.
- Returning a pointer to a struct solely to allow a `nil` "not found"
  value where a `(T, bool)` or a named error (`ErrNotFound`) return is
  clearer and avoids nil-pointer-dereference risk at the call site.
- Panic used for ordinary control flow (a missing map key, a failed
  lookup); reserve `panic` for programmer errors that should crash during
  development (a violated invariant, an unreachable `default` case), and
  return an `error` for everything a caller could reasonably handle.
